    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why it matters</p>
      <h2>A berm that can't hold the tank is three problems at once.</h2>
      <p>Secondary containment is where a tank problem stays a maintenance item instead of becoming a release. Undersized or degraded berms expose you on every front — and the failure mode is quiet until the day it isn't.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div><img class="line-icon" src="/static/img/icons/risk-controls.svg" width="40" height="40" alt=""><h3 class="h4">Risk controls</h3><p>Ineffective routine inspections and containment risk could mean oil on the ground and in the water — and remediation, reporting, and reputational cost that dwarfs the price of two more inches of berm.</p></div>
      <div><img class="line-icon" src="/static/img/icons/operational-insurance.svg" width="40" height="40" alt=""><h3 class="h4">Operational &amp; insurance</h3><p>Auditors, insurers, and buyers all ask the same question: can you prove your containment works? A current, exportable evidence packet answers in minutes instead of weeks of scramble.</p></div>
      <div><img class="line-icon" src="/static/img/icons/regulatory-emissions.svg" width="40" height="40" alt=""><h3 class="h4">Regulatory</h3><p>40 CFR Part 112 expects containment sized for the largest tank plus freeboard, an up-to-date plan, and inspection records you can produce. Stale documentation turns a site visit into findings.</p></div>
    </div>
